  "prompt": "Feature: import a pasted Mealie recipe export with its full structure.\n\nThe HTML or JSON import (POST /api/recipes/create/html-or-json and POST /api/recipes/create/html-or-json/stream) treats every JSON paste as schema.org data and runs it through the web scraper. Pasting the JSON shown by the recipe page's JSON editor (the recipe as GET /api/recipes/{slug} returns it, camelCase) or the JSON file inside a Mealie recipe export zip (snake_case) therefore loses the recipe's structure: the foods and units of its ingredients, the titles of its steps and their links to ingredients, its notes, tools and settings.\n\nExpected:\n1. A new module mealie.services.recipe.mealie_export with parse_mealie_export(data: str). It returns the parsed JSON object unchanged when the text is a complete Mealie recipe dump, and None otherwise. It never raises. A dump is a JSON object (surrounding whitespace is fine) with no \"@context\" or \"@type\" key, a non-blank string \"slug\", a \"groupId\" or \"group_id\" key, and a \"recipeIngredient\" or \"recipe_ingredient\" list in which at least one entry is an object with a \"referenceId\" or \"reference_id\" key. Everything else gives None: schema.org or loose JSON, an empty or string-only ingredient list, a JSON array, HTML, malformed or empty text, and text nested deep enough to hit the recursion limit (\"[\" repeated 100000 times, '{\"a\":' repeated 100000 times).\n2. When either endpoint receives a dump, it never reaches the web scraper (create_from_html). It creates a new recipe the way the export zip import (POST /api/recipes/create/zip) does: a new id and slug, the source recipe untouched, foods, units, tags, categories and tools resolved or created in the importing user's group (a dump from another group works and leaves that group's recipe and organizers as they were), and settings.public taken from the household's recipePublic preference, not from the dump. The rest is kept: description, recipeYield, recipeServings, orgURL, extras, nutrition, the other settings, notes, each ingredient's title, quantity, unit, food and note, and each step's title, summary, text and ingredientReferences (pointing at the new recipe's ingredients).\n3. include_tags false imports no tags and include_categories false no categories. Tools are always kept.\n4. The dump's image, assets and comments are dropped: a paste carries no files, and comments belong to the source recipe. A comment that would not validate must not fail the import.\n5. The plain endpoint answers 201 with the new slug. The stream endpoint sends a \"done\" event with it.\n6. A dump that cannot be imported (for example an ingredient quantity of \"not a number\") does not fall back to the scraper. The plain endpoint answers 400 with detail.message, and the stream endpoint sends an \"error\" event with data.message, both the translated message \"This looks like a recipe exported from Mealie, but it could not be imported\".\n\nOther pastes are handled as before. The paste page should show that message (the frontend is not graded).\n\nTests: `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider tests/unit_tests/services_tests tests/integration_tests/user_recipe_tests` for quick loops; the whole backend suite is `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider tests` (about 8 minutes).",
  "title": "feat: import a pasted Mealie recipe export with its full structure (#8651)",
